se", -2.8729851692332886e-05], ["case", -11.47470474243164]]}, {"t": "?", "lp": -0.6449359059333801, "top": [["?", -0.6449359059333801], [",", -1.508789300918579]]}, {"t": "", "lp": -6.47327397018671e-05, "top": [["", -6.47327397018671e-05], ["\n\n", -9.961883544921875]]}], "text": "# CCS Modes on Intel Arc (xe driver)\n\nThe `ccs_mode` parameter controls how Compute Slice (CCS) contexts are handled by the Xe driver. Here's what each mode does:\n\n## CCS Mode 1\n- **Behavior**: Uses a **dedicated CCS** (Compute Compute Slice) for hardware contexts\n- Contexts are pinned to specific CCS hardware\n- Provides more predictable isolation between contexts\n\n## CCS Mode 2\n- **Behavior**: Uses a **shared CCS** across hardware contexts\n- Multiple contexts can share the same CCS hardware\n- More flexible resource utilization\n\n## CCS Mode 4\n- **Behavior**: Uses **CCS0 specifically** (the first/primary CCS)\n- Forces contexts onto CCS0\n\n## When to use each\n\n**Mode 1** is often the default and recommended for most workloads, especially when you want:\n- Better isolation between different GPU tasks\n- More consistent performance for compute-heavy workloads\n\n**Mode 2** can be useful when:\n- You have many lightweight contexts competing for resources\n- You want to maximize CCS utilization efficiency\n\n**Mode 4** is typically used for:\n- **Debugging** or troubleshooting specific CCS-related issues\n- When you need to pin work to a specific CCS for reproducibility\n- Testing or benchmarking specific hardware paths\n\n---\n\n**A note of caution:** The exact semantics of these modes can vary between driver versions and hardware generations, and documentation for these driver parameters isn't always fully public. If you're hitting a specific problem (performance issues, crashes, or compatibility concerns), it would help to know:\n\n- What driver version are you running?\n- What workload or symptom prompted this question?\n\nThat context would let me give you more targeted advice. Are you working on a particular use case?"}